  "answer": "The code's win condition is met: five settings on five different scenes (S1, S4, S13, S23, S30) failed in every repeat, so each failure rate is surely above one half. But the video and log triage shows these are not five cases of VaVAM rear-ending a braking lead car. In S1 and S30, and in one S23 run, the front contact came at or just after handoff (when the policy takes control from the replayed recording), while the ego was still on the recorded human path. Those contacts come from the replay and the actor boxes, not from the policy. In S4 and S13 the policy did fail, but by drifting left into a vehicle passing in the next lane, not by hitting the car ahead. S13 also used 400 ms of planner delay, above the brief's 200 ms cap. The closest thing to a real lead-vehicle failure is S23: a slow (0.5x) lead car shifted 1.0 s earlier, with 50 ms delay. There the policy took over with a short, closing gap, braked, and still hit the pickup's rear in two runs, but whether the policy was at fault is unclear. With three runs per setting, even these rates are only loosely bounded.",
